  <heading level="2">Simplify Expressions Using the Power Property for Exponents</heading>
  <paragraph>Now let’s look at an exponential expression that contains a power raised to a power. See if you can discover a general property.</paragraph>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>What does this mean?How many factors altogether?</cell>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>So we have</cell>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Notice that 6 is the product of the exponents, 2 and 3.</cell>
        <cell/>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <paragraph>We write:</paragraph>
  <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{matrix} \left( x^{2} \right)^{3} \\ x^{2 \cdot 3} \\ x^{6} \end{matrix}$$</paragraph>
  <paragraph>We multiplied the exponents. This leads to the Power Property for Exponents.</paragraph>
  <note type="rule" source_type="Power Property for Exponents" title="Power Property for Exponents">
    <paragraph>If $$a$$ is a real number, and $$m \text{and} n$$ are whole numbers, then</paragraph>
    <paragraph math="block">$${(a^{m})}^{n} = a^{m \cdot n}$$</paragraph>
    <paragraph>To raise a power to a power, multiply the exponents.</paragraph>
  </note>
  <paragraph>An example with numbers helps to verify this property.</paragraph>
  <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{rll} \left( 3^{2} \right)^{3} &amp; \overset{?}{=} &amp; 3^{2 \cdot 3} \\ (9)^{3} &amp; \overset{?}{=} &amp; 3^{6} \\ 729 &amp; = &amp; {729 \text{✓} } \end{array}$$</paragraph>
  <example number="6.22">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: ⓐ $${(y^{5})}^{9}$$ ⓑ $${(4^{4})}^{7}.$$</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <paragraph>ⓐ</paragraph>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Use the power property, (am)n = am·n.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <paragraph>ⓑ</paragraph>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Use the power property.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="6.43">
    <paragraph>Simplify: ⓐ $${(b^{7})}^{5}$$ ⓑ $${(5^{4})}^{3}.$$</paragraph>
  </note>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="6.44">
